{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1951 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "54dbfde22d3ee0bb8f063021b87a6f8caf2ac4fde037c09c025eb5087b3eb708" :edb/db-before-t 1950 :edb/db-after-t 1951 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535263 50 #inst "2026-10-08T20:44:38.550Z" 13194139535263 true] [17592186179709 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340503 13194139535263 true] [17592186179709 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340510 13194139535263 true] [17592186179710 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340517 13194139535263 true] [17592186179710 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340524 13194139535263 true] [17592186180033 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535263 true] [17592186180033 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" 13194139535263 true] [17592186180033 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $3kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(3t^{2} + 1)m/s^{2}$, where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x$, and $t \\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t = 1s$ and $t = 2s$." 13194139535263 false] [17592186180033 1145 "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int (3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & ={t}^{3}+t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0={0}^{3}+0+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}+t.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)={1}^{3}+1=2\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)={2}^{3}+2=10\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {10}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =150-6 \\\\  & =144\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340503 1017 #edb/ref 17592186340504 13194139535263 true] [17592186340503 1089 "q-163659" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340503 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535263 true] [17592186340503 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's acceleration is $(4t+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=3\\,m/s$ when $t=0\\,s,$ calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340503 1113 #uuid "e3727b71-f77c-52d3-b736-d8fbf4a3880a" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340503 1145 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=4t+1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (4t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}+t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n3=2(0)^{2}+0+C\\,⟹\\,C=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{2}+t+3.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=2(1)^{2}+1+3=6\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=2(2)^{2}+2+3=13\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {13}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {6}^{2} \\\\  & =338-72 \\\\  & =266\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340504 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340505 13194139535263 true] [17592186340504 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340506 13194139535263 true] [17592186340504 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340507 13194139535263 true] [17592186340504 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340508 13194139535263 true] [17592186340504 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340509 13194139535263 true] [17592186340504 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535263 true] [17592186340504 1041 "selection" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340504 1104 #edb/ref 17592186340506 13194139535263 true] [17592186340504 1110 #uuid "a7b152d2-f454-5863-ab30-b02fb89044a1" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340505 1034 "246\\,J" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340505 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535263 true] [17592186340505 1111 #uuid "df69aac9-c7c5-54d5-824a-bf994c63aba9" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340506 1034 "266\\,J" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340506 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535263 true] [17592186340506 1111 #uuid "2336017e-af89-5268-80d1-3366e68f6c33" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340507 1034 "236\\,J" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340507 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535263 true] [17592186340507 1111 #uuid "5daf8589-30ff-57e7-972a-ac9a860b0ca3" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340508 1034 "226\\,J" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340508 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535263 true] [17592186340508 1111 #uuid "4284d4c2-1466-52c7-9cc6-11f30e131536" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340509 1034 "256\\,J" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340509 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535263 true] [17592186340509 1111 #uuid "f3f6e6d2-e38b-5bfc-930e-be3cffb1e523" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340510 1017 #edb/ref 17592186340511 13194139535263 true] [17592186340510 1089 "q-163673" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340510 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535263 true] [17592186340510 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $1.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $5t\\,m/s,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=2\\,s$ and $t=4\\,s.$" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340510 1113 #uuid "e4592e02-6784-5c14-ac84-e06efe25989a" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340510 1145 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=5t,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(2)=5(2)=10\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=4$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(4)=5(4)=20\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1.5\\cdot (20)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1.5\\cdot (10)^{2} \\\\  & =300-75 \\\\  & =225\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340511 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340512 13194139535263 true] [17592186340511 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340513 13194139535263 true] [17592186340511 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340514 13194139535263 true] [17592186340511 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340515 13194139535263 true] [17592186340511 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340516 13194139535263 true] [17592186340511 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535263 true] [17592186340511 1041 "selection" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340511 1104 #edb/ref 17592186340513 13194139535263 true] [17592186340511 1110 #uuid "84bdd8e3-48b4-5c45-8734-a5b03f856bd9" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340512 1034 "150\\,J" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340512 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535263 true] [17592186340512 1111 #uuid "ab5d3f92-ebea-584a-8e8b-e995603ca420" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340513 1034 "225\\,J" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340513 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535263 true] [17592186340513 1111 #uuid "b6805279-2beb-57c4-9a44-4dba5a9e7bc1" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340514 1034 "245\\,J" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340514 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535263 true] [17592186340514 1111 #uuid "5d5f901d-37ac-5a1b-8b4b-bbf11e937b61" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340515 1034 "200\\,J" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340515 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535263 true] [17592186340515 1111 #uuid "96767dfb-287f-53bb-9196-e9d620e98a76" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340516 1034 "175\\,J" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340516 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535263 true] [17592186340516 1111 #uuid "7b85815e-ed10-5368-b547-e190b1eb3114" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340517 1017 #edb/ref 17592186340518 13194139535263 true] [17592186340517 1089 "q-163692" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340517 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535263 true] [17592186340517 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $6\\sqrt{x}\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ at $x=1,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=4.$" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340517 1113 #uuid "46c3ee13-bd0d-5a31-9035-b0ebb0177f31" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340517 1145 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}6\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{x}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =4(8-1) \\\\  & =28\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ at $x=1.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{1}$ at $x=4$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 28 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\ 28 & =\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{1}^{2}-4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}_{1}^{2} & =32 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =64 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=4$ is $8\\,m/s.$" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340519 13194139535263 true] [17592186340518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340520 13194139535263 true] [17592186340518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340521 13194139535263 true] [17592186340518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340522 13194139535263 true] [17592186340518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340523 13194139535263 true] [17592186340518 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535263 true] [17592186340518 1041 "selection" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340518 1104 #edb/ref 17592186340520 13194139535263 true] [17592186340518 1110 #uuid "f453a824-002a-5487-8583-ac98dfe0cb53" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340519 1034 "2\\,m/s" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340519 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535263 true] [17592186340519 1111 #uuid "a50b68df-0019-5469-846f-b2424c0dcbe9" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340520 1034 "8\\,m/s" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340520 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535263 true] [17592186340520 1111 #uuid "9b9f6f27-1240-5e7f-9044-52f1a02f2ac8" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340521 1034 "6\\,m/s" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340521 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535263 true] [17592186340521 1111 #uuid "190ec5a7-8470-5e0b-b466-5dec532fa401" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340522 1034 "12\\,m/s" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340522 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535263 true] [17592186340522 1111 #uuid "7faded0d-dd94-5361-9599-dca0e97eba37" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340523 1034 "4\\,m/s" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340523 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535263 true] [17592186340523 1111 #uuid "e7d1654d-5a4f-5961-b207-5cd2d79460bf" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340524 1017 #edb/ref 17592186340525 13194139535263 true] [17592186340524 1089 "q-163701" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340524 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535263 true] [17592186340524 1100 "A particle $P$ of mass $5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $3x\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters, the particle is initially at $x=1,$ and the particle has velocity $v=5\\,m/s$ at $x=4,$ find the particle's change in velocity in moving from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340524 1113 #uuid "4f55f932-ebf2-5609-b332-0f80ccbf81ff" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340524 1145 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =24-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{2}\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=5\\,m/s$ at $x=4.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=1$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{45}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot {5}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{45}{2} & =\\frac{125}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=1$ is $4\\,m/s.$\n\nFinally, the particle's change in velocity from $x=1$ to $x=4$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}-{v}_{0}=5-4=1\\,m/s.\n$$" 13194139535263 true] [17592186340525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340526 13194139535263 true] [17592186340525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340527 13194139535263 true] [17592186340525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340528 13194139535263 true] [17592186340525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340529 13194139535263 true] [17592186340525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340530 13194139535263 true] [17592186340525 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535263 true] [17592186340525 1041 "selection" 13194139535263 true] 